/*! \page architecture_overview Architecture Overview

The NRP Core framework (NRP-core) enables users to design and execute modular simulations, where multiple modules run concurrently—whether in separate processes, containers, machines, or any combination thereof. These modules, referred to as Engines, synchronize at user-defined frequencies to produce a coherent simulation.

A typical NRP-core experiment consists of multiple engine processes synchronizing with a central Simulation Loop process. Synchronization frequencies, defined by the user in simulation time, set the duration of each engine's independent simulation step (engine time step). Note the following:

- Each engine may have a unique engine time step, defined explicitly within the experiment configuration.
- The internal simulator timestep of an Engine may differ from the engine time step but should be a divisor of the engine time step to maintain consistency.

Detailed information about the synchronization approach can be found in the dedicated \subpage sync_model_details "Synchronization Model".

NRP-core also supports an asynchronous mode, enabling interaction with external simulation modules or processes without strict synchronization. This asynchronous approach is particularly suitable for soft real-time applications and is managed by the EventLoop component, described in-depth on the \subpage event_loop "EventLoop Page".

The NRP-core framework allows easy integration of additional Engines via the \subpage plugin_system "Engine Plugins System". This plugin-based architecture enables users to extend and customize simulation capabilities seamlessly.

Engines within NRP-core experiments can run in separate processes, containers, or distributed across different machines. Engine spawning and execution are controlled by:

- Process Launchers: Managed by the \subpage process_launcher "Process Launchers".
- Launch Commands: Define actual spawning behavior (e.g., separate processes, containers, or machines), described further on the \subpage launch_command "Launch Commands Page".

The core functionality of the NRP is encapsulated within NRPCoreSim, a standalone application built upon NRP-core. NRPCoreSim executes simulations following the same \subpage experiment_lifecycle "Experiment Lifecycle", though without additional features of the complete NRP environment, such as front-end interfaces. Further details about NRPCoreSim can be explored on the \subpage nrp_simulation "NRPCoreSim Page".

Comprehensive details on the main components participating in the NRP-core architecture are outlined \subpage main_elements "here".

To learn more about the workflows supported by NRP-core, refer to the \subpage supported_workflows "Supported Workflows Page".

*/

